\name{ScaleOutOrder}
\alias{ScaleOutOrder}
\title{A bracket with several targets that take the position off in tranches, and a stop that moves to breakeven}
\description{
The targets share the position between them, and only the stop shrinks as each one fills, because the remaining targets already add up to what is left. Once \code{breakeven_after} targets have filled, the stop is moved to the average entry price rather than cancelled and replaced, so there is never a moment without a stop at the exchange. By default UBI holds a limit entry in its virtual order book until the other side of the book reaches its price, answering HTTP 202 with an \code{outcome} of \code{armed}, while the exits rest at the broker as the entry fills; give \code{hold_limits} \code{FALSE} to send the entry at once. The breakeven stop is rounded to the tick on the side that cannot lose, and a target or stop off the tick is refused with HTTP 400 when the order is placed, and by a dry run as well. An exit the broker refuses cancels the rest of the entry, and the parent ends \code{failed}, because the position is left without that exit.

The order template's attributes are described on \code{SyntheticOrder}.
}
